    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe feature is wired cleanly and most of it follows the grain of this codebase well: the `Owner.capacityWarning` transient field mirrors the existing `bulkSignupWarning` / `possibleDuplicate` flags exactly, `FlagCityCapacityWarning` follows the `Flag*` single-`service`-method step convention (like `FlagPossibleDuplicate`), the YAML steps mirror `flagPossibleDuplicate`'s placement with the correct `transaction` / `readonly-transaction` governance on each pipeline, and the OpenAPI entry matches the neighboring read-only flags. The one structural problem is where the shared counting logic and its constant now live.\n\nFindings:\n\n1. **`countInCity` is in the wrong unit.** The shared per-city count loop was extracted onto the function class `CheckCityCapacity` as a `static` method, and `FlagCityCapacityWarning` now reaches across into a sibling function class to call it. That is against the established pattern here: function classes are single-step units (one `service` method), and cross-step shared comparison/aggregation logic lives in dedicated helper classes \u2014 `OwnerCity`, `Household`, `OwnerAddress`. Note `FlagPossibleDuplicate` reuses `Household.normalizePostcode`, not a static on another step. `OwnerCity` already exists specifically for city normalization/comparison and is the natural home: move `countInCity` onto `OwnerCity` (e.g. `OwnerCity.countInCity(city, ownerRepository)`), so both `CheckCityCapacity` and `FlagCityCapacityWarning` depend on the helper rather than on each other. This keeps the step classes pure and puts the count next to the normalization it relies on.\n\n2. **`WARNING_THRESHOLD` is misplaced.** It is defined on `CheckCityCapacity` (the hard-rejection class) but is used solely by `FlagCityCapacityWarning`. The warning threshold is the warning's concern; placing it on the hard-check class \u2014 and having the warning class pull both `WARNING_THRESHOLD` and `CAPACITY` from there \u2014 reinforces the step-to-step coupling in (1). Keep `CAPACITY` where the hard check reads it, and put `WARNING_THRESHOLD` on `FlagCityCapacityWarning` (it can still reference `CheckCityCapacity.CAPACITY`, or move the shared limit to `OwnerCity` alongside the count).\n\n3. **Stale helper javadoc (minor).** `OwnerCity`'s class comment asserts \"there is a single consumer (`CheckCityCapacity`).\" The count is now consumed by the warning path as well; if the count logic moves into `OwnerCity` per (1) this comment should be updated, otherwise it is left inaccurate.",
